---
/**
 * The Archive → Work Entry dissolve — TECHNICAL_ARCHITECTURE.md §13.
 *
 * OWNERSHIP: shared. A component with no markup, because what it carries is a
 * document-level opt-in and nothing else.
 *
 * §13, verbatim: "**Archive → Work Entry dissolve:** CSS `@view-transition
 * { navigation: auto }` — a *cross-document* transition requiring no router.
 * Astro's `ClientRouter` is **not** the mechanism… The CSS form ships zero JS and
 * degrades to plain navigation where unsupported."
 *
 * ── WHY THIS IS A COMPONENT AND NOT A LINE IN A STYLESHEET ─────────────────
 * `@view-transition` opts in the whole DOCUMENT, and `global.css` is loaded by
 * every page — putting the rule there would opt the entire site into a
 * transition §13 scopes to one journey. As a component style it ships only in
 * the stylesheet of the pages that compose it, which is exactly the two halves
 * of that journey: the archive family and the Work Entry.
 *
 * ── WHY ONE COMPONENT AND NOT TWO ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * A cross-document transition requires BOTH documents to opt in, so the rule is
 * inherently a *pair*. It was authored twice — once as `work-archive/
 * ArchiveDissolve.astro` when the archive shipped its half, and again inline in
 * `work-entry/WorkEntryPage.astro` when the Work Entry shipped the other — each
 * with the other's file out of reach in a parallel workstream. The Work Entry's
 * own comment recorded that as "a one-line follow-up flagged in the report".
 * This is that follow-up: the two identical declarations became one shared
 * component at the A3 + A4 integration. Nothing about the rendered result
 * changed — the same six lines still ship, to the same pages.
 *
 * ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * The HiFi's exit animation is a script: it calls `preventDefault()`, adds a
 * `leaving` class and navigates with `setTimeout(…, 430)`. That must not survive
 * — it breaks middle-click, modifier-click and the browser's own navigation
 * handling, and it makes a 430 ms delay load-bearing on every card
 * (VISUAL_DIRECTION_v2.0 §12: "never make a single motion load-bearing"). The
 * CSS form produces the same result with no JS and no intercepted click.
 *
 * Named transition parts (`view-transition-name` pairing the clicked card's
 * image with the entry's hero, so it morphs rather than crossfades) are NOT set.
 * A name on one side only animates a lone element rather than morphing a pair,
 * so naming belongs to the two sides being changed together — now that both
 * sides are here, that is a single, deliberate follow-up rather than a
 * cross-workstream one.
 *
 * MOTION_NOTES.md:62 requires layout parity under reduced motion, and
 * `WORK_ARCHIVE_IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md`:45 is explicit for this transition:
 * "Reduced-motion falls back to native navigation." Killing the animations
 * yields exactly that — the same navigation, cut rather than crossfaded.
 *
 * §14.0 binds it and this satisfies the binding: the rule ships zero JS, an
 * unsupporting browser ignores the at-rule and performs a plain document load,
 * and navigation never depends on it.
 */
---

<style is:global>
  @view-transition {
    navigation: auto;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    ::view-transition-group(*),
    ::view-transition-old(*),
    ::view-transition-new(*) {
      animation: none !important;
    }
  }
</style>
